		'option_d'       => 'Ask which parent has the greater amount of physical parenting time',
		'correct_option' => 'a',
		'explanation'    => 'A. Correct.
		“Joint custody” is not sufficiently specific. California defines joint legal custody as both parents sharing the right and responsibility to make decisions concerning the child’s health, education, and welfare. The custody order must specify the circumstances in which both parents’ consent is required; in circumstances not requiring mutual consent, either parent may act alone.
		The therapist therefore needs to review the actual order instead of relying on a parent’s summary or a general assumption about joint custody.
		Why the Other Answers Are Incorrect
		B. Incorrect. Joint legal custody does not invariably mean that both parents must consent to every healthcare decision. The language of the order controls.
		C. Incorrect. Attendance at the appointment does not establish the full scope of the parent’s legal authority.
		D. Incorrect. Physical parenting time and legal decision-making authority are different concepts. The parent with more physical custody does not automatically possess sole authority over treatment.
		CTA Exam Strategy: When the stem mentions:DivorceSeparationJoint custodyA custody disputeA court orderlook for:Review the actual current order.Do not guess from labels such as “joint,” “primary,” or “custodial parent.”
		Remediation: Return to Workbook 1, Chapter 5: General Consent Requirements for Minors; review Reviewing Custody Orders; log error category MINOR-AUTHORITY.',
