		For a verbal update, the therapist must separately consider:
		Who authorized treatment
		The confidentiality arrangement
		The parent’s treatment role
		Any applicable authorization
		Whether disclosure is required or permitted by law
		Whether safety information must be communicated
		The amount of information reasonably necessary
		California generally requires authorization before medical information is disclosed unless a statutory exception applies.
		A. Incorrect. The record-access statute does not prohibit all parent involvement or communication.
		C. Incorrect. Requesting information verbally does not create unlimited access to confidential session content.
		D. Incorrect. Confidentiality protections apply to medical information regardless of whether it is spoken, written, or electronically maintained.
		CTA Exam Strategy: Ask exactly what the parent is requesting: Participation? A progress update? A release? Inspection? Copies? Different requests may require different legal analyses.
