		'explanation'    => 'C. Correct.
		The minor may authorize disclosure of information obtained during services to which the minor could lawfully consent. However, the authorization must satisfy statutory requirements. These include identifying:
		The types of information to be disclosed
		The provider permitted to disclose it
		The recipient
		The authorized uses and limitations
		An expiration date or event
		The authorization must also be signed, dated, and otherwise properly executed. A vague, unlimited, permanent release does not satisfy these safeguards.
		A. Incorrect. A signature alone does not cure missing statutory elements.
		B. Incorrect. An authorization must contain an appropriate expiration date or event rather than remaining indefinitely effective merely because the client is a minor.
		D. Incorrect. The parent ordinarily cannot authorize disclosure of information obtained during services the minor was legally entitled to consent to independently.
		CTA Exam Strategy: A valid release is not just: “The client signed something.” Check scope, recipient, use, expiration, signature, and date.
