	array(
		'question_text'  => 'A mature 16-year-old independently consented to outpatient counseling under California law. The following events occur: The noncustodial parent requests the complete record. No court order limits that parent’s general parental rights. The minor also requests a personal copy of the records. The therapist determines that direct access by the minor would create a substantial risk of significant adverse psychological consequences. The minor designates a licensed psychologist to receive and review the records. What should the therapist do?',
		'option_a'       => 'Release the complete record to the parent because noncustodial status cannot be used to deny access',
		'option_b'       => 'Deny both requests without explanation because the records involve psychotherapy',
		'option_c'       => 'Release the records directly to the minor despite the identified substantial risk',
		'option_d'       => 'Deny the parent access to the independently authorized treatment records, follow the required protective procedures regarding the minor’s request, and permit the designated licensed psychologist to inspect or receive the records',
		'correct_option' => 'd',
		'explanation'    => 'D. Correct.
		The parent’s and minor’s requests involve different statutory rights.
		The Parent’s Request
		Family Code section 3025 provides that records cannot be denied solely because a parent is noncustodial. However, Health and Safety Code section 123115 separately provides that a minor’s representative is not entitled to records relating to qualifying services the minor lawfully authorized independently.
		The independent-consent restriction—not the parent’s noncustodial status—is the basis for denying this request.
		The Minor’s Request
		A minor lawfully authorized to consent to the service generally has a right to inspect and obtain the records relating to that type of care.
		Because the therapist has identified a substantial risk of significant adverse psychological consequences, the therapist may restrict direct access only by following the safeguards in section 123115. These include:
		Documenting the request
		Explaining the specific anticipated consequences in the record
		Informing the minor of the refusal
		Informing the minor of the right to designate a qualifying licensed professional
		Providing the records to the licensed professional designated by the minor
		Documenting whether that option was requested
		The designated professional may review the records but may not permit the minor to inspect or copy them directly through that process.
		A. Incorrect. Section 3025 prevents denial based solely on noncustodial status, but it does not override the independent-minor-consent restriction in section 123115.
		B. Incorrect. The therapist must provide the required explanation and professional-review option rather than imposing an undocumented blanket denial.
		C. Incorrect. The therapist may restrict direct access when the statutory substantial-risk standard and procedural safeguards are satisfied.
